# Line endings are pinned deliberately, not left to each developer's git settings.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on at least one machine in this project, which rewrites line
# endings on checkout. For most files that is harmless. For the files below it is not, and
# one of those cases already cost four full build-push-test cycles to diagnose: a
# `tokens.txt` with CRLF leaves a stray carriage return attached to the last field, and
# sherpa-onnx does not report an error for that — it aborts the process in native code with
# no message at all. See the note in tools/export-mms-tts.py.

# Default: let git normalise text in the repository, and leave binaries alone.
* text=auto

# --- Must be LF, because something other than a text editor parses them ---------------

# Read by sherpa-onnx's C++ readers, which split on \n only. CRLF here is a native abort.
tokens.txt text eol=lf
*/tokens.txt text eol=lf

# Phrase codebooks. Kotlin's readLines tolerates CRLF and PhraseCodebook strips it, so
# this is defence in depth rather than a known break — but the file's line numbers are a
# wire contract, and nothing about it should vary by who cloned it.
phrases.txt text eol=lf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF: the interpreter on the shebang line gets a
# trailing \r and the script fails with a confusing "not found".
gradlew text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# --- Must be CRLF ----------------------------------------------------------------------

# Windows-only, and PowerShell/cmd are the consumers.
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# --- Binary: never normalise, never diff as text --------------------------------------

*.onnx binary
*.aar binary
*.jar binary
*.apk binary
*.tflite binary
*.wav binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.pptx binary
